                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="487-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Carvin Corporation CM-87S</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The CM-87S is large-diaphragm condenser based on the ADK A51 / MXL 2001. (Though previously identified on this page as a &#8220;Chinese clone of the Neumann U87,&#8221; it is no such thing; its circuit and capsule differ significantly.)</p><p>It shares body parts and perhaps internal components with numerous Alctron and Soundking products, such as Alctron&#8217;s HSMC003 and the Soundking EC011. However, full specs for all these products are not available, preventing an accurate identification.</p><p>This mic comes with a dedicated phantom-power supply. </p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/carvin/carvin-87.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/carvin/carvin-87-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="CM-87S" width="133" /></a>The mic&#8217;s circuit appears to be identical to the A51/2001; this is a hybrid design, with a transistor stage preceding a 2:1 output transformer. The capsule is presumably a 32mm K67 style.</p><p><span class="photocredit">Circuit photos contributed by J. Bunyaner</span></p><p>The mic ships in an aluminum flightcase with a 30&#39; cable, power supply and shockmount.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Carvin/CM-87S">Carvin Corporation CM-87S</a> is also known as: CM87, CM87S.</p>
